WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.960
A practical cloud IAM workflow starts with inventory.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:08.500
List human users, groups, roles, service accounts, managed identities,

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:14.800
workload identities, applications, API keys, tokens, temporary credential patterns,

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:16.620
and emergency accounts.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:22.040
Assign owners and classify whether the access is human, workload, automation,

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.020
administrative, production, development, or third-party.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.640
If an identity has no owner or purpose, it is already a governance problem.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:34.380
Next, define tasks and grant least privilege.

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:39.400
Start from what the person or workload needs to do, map required actions to resources,

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.820
scope permissions narrowly, use conditions where appropriate,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.040
and separate duties for sensitive work.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:49.500
Avoid broad administrator, owner, contributor, wildcard,

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.400
and full-access permissions unless there is a clear, approved reason.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.300
Review effective access, not only the most visible role assignment.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.720
Use federation and MFA for human access where possible.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.940
Prefer temporary credentials, role assumption, just-in-time access,

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:08.360
and managed identity patterns over long-lived secrets.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.300
Protect workload identities with narrow deployment roles, environment separation,

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.080
secret rotation, and auditability.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:21.420
Treat CI/CD pipelines, containers, virtual machines, serverless functions,

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.840
and service accounts as first-class IAM subjects.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.100
Finally, monitor and review continuously.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.600
Log IAM activity and cloud API usage.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:37.100
Watch for policy changes, key creation, trust relationship changes, role passing,

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.160
impersonation, denied access, unusual API calls,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:44.020
and changes to logging or security controls.

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:48.480
Review access regularly, remove stale identities, track exceptions,

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.580
protect emergency accounts, and measure progress.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:56.600
Secure cloud IAM depends on identity lifecycle, permission design, monitoring,

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.580
and governance working together.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.840
A compact review checklist helps keep the practice repeatable.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:03.980
Can every identity be explained?

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.200
Does every privileged role have an owner?

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:08.660
Are human users federated where possible?

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.320
Are workloads using short-lived or managed identity patterns?

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:15.260
Are long-lived keys justified and monitored?

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.200
Are sensitive IAM changes logged and reviewed?

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.260
Are stale grants removed quickly?

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.160
The final lesson is that cloud IAM is never finished.

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:30.220
New projects, pipelines, vendors, identities, and services appear constantly.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:33.320
Secure programs build review into the operating rhythm.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:37.700
They make safe access easy to request, risky access visible,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.240
and stale access easy to remove.

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:45.580
That is how IAM becomes a durable cloud security foundation instead of a growing list of

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:46.920
exceptions.
